<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Turks and Caicos Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1848 CE – present · capital Cockburn Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Low-lying islands southeast of the Bahamas, once a centre of salt raking, now a British Overseas Territory dependent on tourism and finance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bermudian salt rakers worked the Turks Islands from the 1680s, and Loyalists from the American colonies planted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caicos-islands">Caicos</a> after 1783. The islands were administered from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bahamas">Bahamas</a> from 1799, but salt merchants on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-turk-island">Grand Turk</a> resented Nassau's taxes. In 1848 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> made them a separate colony with their own president and council under the oversight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica's</a> governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The economy rested almost entirely on salt, raked from shallow pans by enslaved and then free labourers and exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The tiny colony struggled to pay its way, and in 1873 it was annexed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a> as a dependency, with a commissioner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cockburn-town">Cockburn Town</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-turk-island">Grand Turk</a>. A US missile tracking station later operated on Grand Turk, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-glenn">John Glenn</a> landed nearby in 1962.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a> became independent in 1962, the islands became a separate crown colony, sharing the Bahamas' governor until 1973. They remain a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territory</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> suspended self-government from 2009 to 2012 after corruption findings. Tourism on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/providenciales">Providenciales</a> is now the main industry.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turks-and-caicos-islands.jpg" alt="Turks and Caicos Islands" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Turks_and_Caicos_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1848 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cockburn Town</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turks and Caicos Islands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turks and Caicos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1937&amp;focus=turks-and-caicos-islands" aria-label="Turks and Caicos Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2770 1384 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Turks and Caicos Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3090 1640L4997 651M3090 1640L2949 1511M3090 1640L2917 1766"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M2949 1511h0M2917 1766h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3090 1640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3090 1640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cockburn Town. <a href="/atlas?year=1937&amp;focus=turks-and-caicos-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
